  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "38276632-22e3-5d15-8898-a9df3c113c10",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=2 e^{x} - 2$\n- **II.** $y=2 e^{x}$\n- **III.** $y=-2 + e^{- x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2 e^{x} - 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(2 e^{x} - 2\\right) \\\\ 2 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2 e^{x} - 2 \\\\ 2 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 e^{x} - 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 e^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(2 e^{x}\\right) \\\\ 2 e^{x} & =2 e^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2 e^{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2 + e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ - e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(-2 + e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ - e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 + e^{- x} \\\\ 2 - 2 e^{- x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2 + e^{- x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "5fba8192-bdea-5f2b-9c47-c7c94e189c14",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
